---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
---
<Docs title="Getting started" description="Install Normal UI in any project: plain CSS or React components.">
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <div class="pd-content docs-prose">
    <p>
      Normal UI ships as two npm packages. Both are public and experimental (0.x):
      attributes and names may change before 1.0. MIT licensed. You need React 19
      for the components; the CSS works anywhere.
    </p>
    <h2>Plain HTML or any framework</h2>
    <pre style="overflow-x:auto"><code>pnpm add @imsus/normal-ui-css</code></pre>
<pre style="overflow-x:auto"><code>import '@imsus/normal-ui-css'; // tokens + base styles + patterns
import '@imsus/normal-ui-css/themes/govuk.css'; // optional: govuk, mcmaster, usgraphics</code></pre>
    <p>
      Write semantic elements and they already look right. <code>data-color-scheme="light|dark"</code>
      on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> forces a color scheme (without it, the device decides);
      <code>data-theme="&lt;id&gt;"</code> applies a theme. Override any value with
      your own custom property; your unlayered CSS beats the layers underneath.
    </p>
    <h2>React</h2>
    <pre style="overflow-x:auto"><code>pnpm add @imsus/normal-ui-react</code></pre>
<pre style="overflow-x:auto"><code>import '@imsus/normal-ui-css';
import '@imsus/normal-ui-css/themes/govuk.css'; // optional
import '@imsus/normal-ui-react/styles.css';</code></pre>
<pre style="overflow-x:auto"><code>import {'{'} Alert, Button, TextField {'}'} from '@imsus/normal-ui-react';

&lt;&gt;
  &lt;Alert kind="warning"&gt;3 products are almost out of stock.&lt;/Alert&gt;
  &lt;TextField label="Email address" type="email" autoComplete="email" /&gt;
  &lt;Button type="submit"&gt;Save address&lt;/Button&gt;
&lt;/&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Components take native props plus <code>className</code> and <code>style</code>
      (applied after the component's own styles) and <code>xstyle</code> for StyleX
      users. Interactive components carry <code>'use client'</code>; importing one
      into a server-component page is the client boundary. Works in the last 2
      Chrome, Firefox, and Safari.
    </p>
  </div>
</Docs>
